 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T16:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Probably yes for most people, but check the live reading before you go.** I can't see real-time data, so this is based on the most recent reports I found.\n\n**Latest readings**\n- The Borneo Post's 17 Oct report has Kuching at an API of 65. That falls in Malaysia's \"Moderate\" band. The DOE scale runs 0–50 good, 51–100 moderate, 101–200 unhealthy, 201–300 very unhealthy, and above 300 hazardous.\n- Readings have swung over the past week. Kuching's API had reached an unhealthy 111 before dropping to 85 on 11 Oct. Haze can worsen again quickly, especially in the evening and overnight.\n- International AQI sites show higher numbers for the same air. AQI.in listed a US-scale AQI of 132 (\"Poor\") with PM2.5 at 58 µg/m³ on 15 Oct. The US scale is stricter than Malaysia's API, so the numbers aren't directly comparable.\n\n**Practical guidance**\n- **API under 50:** ride normally.\n- **API 51–100 (moderate):** most healthy adults can ride. Keep the pace easy, because hard efforts make you breathe in much more polluted air. Consider a shorter route.\n- **API over 100:** skip it or ride indoors. Under Malaysia's coordinated procedures, outdoor activities are postponed when the API exceeds 100, though that rule is written for schools.\n- **Asthma, heart or lung conditions, pregnancy, or older age:** be more cautious, and skip riding if the API is above about 50–60.\n- **Masks:** a cloth or surgical mask does little against haze particles. An N95 or KN95 helps but makes hard riding uncomfortable.\n- **Warning signs:** stop if you get a sore throat, coughing, chest tightness, wheezing, or eye irritation.\n\n**Check right before you leave**\n- The DOE's APIMS portal (apims.doe.gov.my) for the official Kuching reading.\n- The NREB Sarawak API status report.\n- The aqicn.org Kuching page, to see whether the trend is rising or falling.\n\nIf the live API is in the 60s and falling, an easy ride should be fine. If it's climbing toward 100, I'd stay in.",
